    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d541">

                <pb facs="0655" n="561" xml:id="pg561"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">812.543 Palmolive Co./5: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to
                        the Ambassador in Mexico</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Morrow</hi>)</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1929-11-07T19:00:00-05:00">November 7, 1929—7
                        p.m.</date></dateline></opener>
                <p>505. Your 351, November 5, 6 p.m. Department is clearly of opinion that under
                    provisions article 2, Washington Convention, any corporation organized under
                    laws of a country party to that convention and owning a trade mark registered in
                    the United States would be permitted to sue in United States Courts for
                    infringement of such trade mark. See <hi rendition="#italic">The French
                        Republic</hi> v. <hi rendition="#italic">Saratoga Vichy Spring Co.</hi>, 191
                    U.S. 427; and <hi rendition="#italic">Baglin</hi> v. <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Cusenier Company</hi>, 221 U. S. 580.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Stimson</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
